## Adding and restructuring pages

Ting may add new pages and sections, and restructure the **page layer**: the static pages, the navigation, and how pages are grouped. This covers `app/pages/*`, the nav and chrome components, and `public/`. It does NOT extend to the locked Shopify-backed routes (products, collections, blog) or any locked file.

**When you rename or remove a page or route, you MUST add a redirect.** Otherwise links people saved, and pages Google has indexed, will hit a dead "page not found". In the same piece of work, before you save:

1. Add an entry to `lib/redirects.ts` sending the old path to the new one (or to the closest matching page).
2. Never edit `next.config.ts` for this. Redirects live in `lib/redirects.ts` on purpose, so adding one can never touch the image settings.

If you are removing a page and there is no sensible new home for it, send the old path to the most relevant remaining page (its parent section, say), never to a dead end.

---

## Systematic changes, not patchwork

When a request applies to a **class** of things, change every member of the class, never just the one in front of you.

- "Add a contact line to the policy pages" means ALL the policy pages, not one.
- "Make the section headings bigger" means everywhere that heading style is used.
- The footer, the nav, a repeated card: if it appears in many places it almost certainly lives in one shared component. Edit the shared component once rather than copy-pasting the same change into several files.

Before you save, ask yourself: are there sibling pages or components of the same kind that should change too? If yes, do them all in the same piece of work, and tell Ting which ones you swept. Leaving one collection page, or one policy page, different from its siblings is a bug, not a saving of effort.

---

## If something looks wrong after going live: roll back

The fastest fix for a bad change that went live is almost never more editing. It is putting the site back to the last good version.

- **The way to roll back is through you (Cursor).** If Ting says something looks wrong, undo the last change that went live with `git revert <last pushed commit>`, then make it live again (push). Tell her plainly: "Done. The site is back to how it was before that change, live within a minute." NEVER use `git reset --hard`.
- **If you cannot roll back safely** (several changes went live together, or the revert will not build), do not guess. Tell Ting "I can't safely undo this myself, please message Ryo" and stop.
- After any rollback, add a one-line note to `docs/cursor-followups.md` with the date and what went wrong, so Ryo can look into it.

(A one-click Vercel dashboard rollback needs the paid Pro plan with Ting added to the Vercel team. On the current free plan that is not available, so Cursor is how Ting rolls back.)

